--- !u!114 &-2349281884924166291
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: 'The baked lighting settings are located in the <i>Lighting</i> window.


      1. Head to <b>Window</b> > <b>Rendering</b>> <b>Lighting.</b>


      2. At the top of the <i>Lighting</i> window, in the <i>Scene</i> tab, open the <b>Mixed Lighting</b> foldout.


      3. Notice that the Lighting Mode is set to <b>Shadowmask,</b> a mode that allows for a mixture of real-time shadowmaps and baked shadows blending based on the camera distance.



      <b>Expert tips</b>


      The distance at which the transition between real-time shadows and Shadowmask blend is controlled in the Render Pipeline Asset assigned to the active Quality Level.


      There are several Lighting Modes, you can learn more about them  and their different use cases in the <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/2022.3/Documentation/Manual/lighting-mode.html">documentation.</a>


      To learn more specifically about Shadowmasks, you can consult the corresponding <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/LightMode-Mixed-Shadowmask.html">documentation.</a>'
--- !u!114 &-1938668891321826500
